e, and which term wins at high temperature?"}, "N_ADDED": {"note": "Their electron count adds the two half-reactions' electrons instead of finding the common multiple.", "ask": "How many electrons does each half-reaction have to give or take so that the counts match?"}, "NOT_LOWEST_WHOLE_NUMBERS": {"note": "Their coefficients balance but are not the lowest whole numbers.", "ask": "Is there a number every coefficient can be divided by?"}, "ANODE_CATHODE_SWAPPED": {"note": "Their cell potential has the anode and cathode the wrong way round.", "ask": "Which electrode is oxidised, and which potential is subtracted from which?"}, "POTENTIALS_ADDED": {"note": "Their value adds the two standard potentials.", "ask": "Does the relation add the potentials or take their difference?"}, "N_WRONG": {"note": "Their value uses the wrong number of electrons.", "ask": "How many electrons pass for the equation as written?"}, "LN_FOR_LOG": {"note": "Their value uses the natural logarithm where the base-10 form with 0.0592 V was needed, or the reverse.", "ask": "Which logarithm goes with 0.0592 V?"}, "N_DROPPED": {"note": "Their Nernst value leaves out n.", "ask": "What divides 0.0592 V in the relation?"}, "NERNST_SIGN": {"note": "Their Nernst value adds the correction instead of subtracting it.", "ask": "What sign sits between E° and the logarithm term?"}, "TIME_NOT_CONVERTED": {"note": "Their charge uses the time in hours or minutes where seconds were needed.", "ask": "What unit of time does a coulomb per second need?"}, "ELECTRONS_PER_ION_WRONG": {"note": "Their moles of metal use the wrong electrons per ion.", "ask": "How many electrons does each of these ions take to become the metal?"}, "FARADAY_INVERTED": {"note": "Their moles of electrons multiply by F instead of dividing.", "ask": "Does the charge divided by F give moles, or the other way round?"}, "D_COUNT_FROM_GROUP": {"note": "Their d count is the metal's group number, ignoring the oxidation state.", "ask": "How many electrons did the metal lose to reach this oxidation state?"}, "SPIN_STATE_WRONG": {"note": "Their unpaired count belongs to the other spin state.", "ask": "Does this ligand split the orbitals enough to make pairing cheaper?"}, "NM_NOT_CONVERTED": {"note": "Their energy uses the wavelength in nanometres.", "ask": "What unit does λ need in E = hc/λ?"}, "NA_OMITTED": {"note": "Their per-mole value is the per-ion value.", "ask": "How many ions are in a mole?"}, "LAMBDA_INVERTED": {"note": "They computed t½ ÷ ln 2 instead of ln 2 ÷ t½.", "ask": "Should λ get larger or smaller as the half-life grows?"}, "FRACTION_DECAYED": {"note": "They gave the fraction that decayed, not the fraction remaining.", "ask": "Does exp(−λt) give what is left or what is gone?"}, "LINEAR_HALVING": {"note": "They treated decay as linear instead of exponential.", "ask": "Does the same amount, or the same fraction, decay in each half-life?"}, "LOG10_FOR_LN": {"note": "They used log base 10 instead of ln.", "ask": "The equation uses ln. Which key is that?"}, "DAUGHTER_AS_N0": {"note": "They used the daughter's moles as N0 instead of parent now plus parent decayed.", "ask": "What did the rock contain when it formed?"}, "MASS_RATIO_USED": {"note": "They used grams, not moles, for parent and daughter.", "ask": "Parent and daughter have different molar masses. What must you compare?"}, "ELECTRONS_OMITTED": {"note": "Their mass defect leaves out the electrons.", "ask": "Does the atomic mass include the electrons?"}, "AMU_NOT_CONVERTED": {"note": "Their energy uses the mass in amu where kilograms were needed.", "ask": "What unit of mass does E = mc² need?"}, "DIVIDED_BY_Z": {"note": "Their per-nucleon value divides by the protons only.", "ask": "How many nucleons are in the nucleus?"}, "EV_FOR_MEV": {"note": "Their value is in electronvolts where MeV was asked.", "ask": "How many electronvolts are in an MeV?"}, "RATIO_INVERTED": {"note": "Their Nt/N0 is upside down, giving a negative time.", "ask": "Which goes on top: now, or originally?"}}, "trap_notes_by_opener": {}, "equation_symbols": {"E": ["E"], "t": ["t"]}, "equation_constants": {}, "openers": [{"title": "The Cobalt-60 Source", "question": "A hospital's cobalt-60 radiotherapy source is checked each year with a radiation meter, and its reading keeps falling. Cobalt-60's half-life is 5.27 years. Why does the same fraction of the source decay each year, and how much is left after 15 years?", "model_answer": "Decay is first order: λ = ln 2/t½ = 0.693/5.27 y = 0.1315 y⁻¹. Nt/N0 = exp(−λt) = exp(−0.1315 × 15) = 0.139: 13.9% remains. To reach 2.00%: t = −(1/λ) ln 0.0200 = 29.7 y. (OpenStax Example 21.5; the text rounds λ to 0.132 and gets 0.138 and 29.6 y.)", "target": "Radioactive decay is first order: λ = ln 2/t½, Nt/N0 = exp(−λt), and t = −(1/λ) ln(Nt/N0).", "keywords": ["first order", "half-life", "decay constant", "λ = ln 2/t½", "exponential", "same fraction", "exp(−λt)", "natural log"], "liquids": ["CS1", "CS2", "CS3", "CS4", "CS5", "CS6"], "kinds": ["fraction", "time"], "fits": ["CS1", "CS2", "CS3", "CS4", "CS5", "CS6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch21/Cobalt60_source.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch21/decay_simulator.html?isotope={isotope}&t={t}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["radioactive decay", "half-life", "decay constant", "first-order kinetics"], "role": "worked", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Hospital Isotope Shelf", "question": "A hospital nuclear-medicine lab keeps several radioisotopes, each with its own half-life, from fluorine-18's 110 minutes to cobalt-60's 5.27 years. A dose prepared in the morning must be checked before use. How do you find how much of a sample is left after a given time?", "model_answer": "λ = ln 2/t½ (same time unit as t); Nt/N0 = exp(−λt); mass left = (Nt/N0) × m0. Example: iodine-131, t½ = 8.040 d, 2.50 g after 14.0 d: λ = 0.08621 d⁻¹, Nt/N0 = 0.299, 0.748 g left.", "target": "Fraction remaining = exp(−λt), with λ = ln 2/t½ in matching time units.", "keywords": ["half-life", "decay constant", "fraction remaining", "exponential", "same time unit", "mass left", "first order"], "liquids": ["HI1", "HI2", "HI3", "HI4", "HI5", "HI6"], "kinds": ["fraction"], "fits": ["HI1", "HI2", "HI3", "HI4", "HI5", "HI6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch21/Hospital_isotope_shelf.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch21/decay_simulator.html?isotope={isotope}&t={t}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["radioactive decay", "half-life", "radioisotope"], "role": "exam", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Dead Sea Scrolls", "question": "A tiny piece of a Dead Sea Scroll gives 10.8 carbon-14 disintegrations per minute per gram of carbon in a counter; living plants give 13.6. How can the activity left in old paper tell its age?", "model_answer": "Activity is proportional to the C-14 left: Nt/N0 = 10.8/13.6 = 0.794. λ = 0.693/5730 y = 1.21 × 10⁻⁴ y⁻¹. t = −(1/λ) ln 0.794 = 1.91 × 10³ y. (OpenStax Example 21.6.)", "target": "Radiocarbon dating: the ratio of present to original activity gives Nt/N0, and t = −(1/λ) ln(Nt/N0).", "keywords": ["carbon-14", "activity", "disintegrations", "proportional", "ratio", "half-life 5730 years", "age", "ln"], "liquids": ["DS1", "DS2", "DS3", "DS4", "DS5", "DS6"], "kinds": ["dating"], "fits": ["DS1", "DS2", "DS3", "DS4", "DS5", "DS6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch21/Dead_sea_scrolls.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch21/decay_simulator.html?isotope={isotope}&t={t}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["radiocarbon dating", "activity", "half-life"], "role": "floater", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Oldest Rocks", "question": "A geologist grinds an igneous rock and a mass spectrometer finds 9.58 × 10⁻⁵ g of uranium-238 and 2.51 × 10⁻⁵ g of lead-206, the end of uranium's decay chain. How can the lead in a rock tell how long ago it formed?", "model_answer": "All the Pb-206 came from U-238: U now = 4.03 × 10⁻⁷ mol; U decayed = Pb = 1.22 × 10⁻⁷ mol; N0 = 5.24 × 10⁻⁷ mol; Nt/N0 = 0.768. λ = 0.693/4.47 × 10⁹ y; t = 1.7 × 10⁹ y. (OpenStax Example 21.7; Appendix M's half-life, 4.47 × 10⁹ y, against the example's 4.5 × 10⁹ y.)", "target": "Rock dating: original parent = parent now + parent decayed (equal to daughter, in moles); then t = −(1/λ) ln(Nt/N0).", "keywords": ["uranium-238", "lead-206", "moles", "daughter", "original amount", "parent now plus decayed", "half-life", "age"], "liquids": ["OR1", "OR2", "OR3", "OR4", "OR5", "OR6"], "kinds": ["rock"], "fits": ["OR1", "OR2", "OR3", "OR4", "OR5", "OR6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch21/Oldest_rocks.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch21/decay_simulator.html?isotope={isotope}&t={t}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["radiometric dating", "parent isotope", "daughter isotope"], "role": "floater", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}], "moves": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "Does a nucleus know how old it is? What does that make the rate of decay depend on?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning radioactive decay and half-life. Explain why decay is first order and what a half-life means. Use an isotope other than mine. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "In your own words, why does the same fraction decay in every half-life?", "park_text": "Each nucleus has the same chance of decaying per unit time, regardless of age, so the number decaying is proportional to the number present: first-order decay. The same fraction disappears in each half-life.", "status": "approved (instructor, 18 Oct 2026)"}, "lambda_found": {"state": "lambda_found", "stage": "calculate", "label_when_done": "λ found", "ask": "Find the decay constant λ = ln 2 ÷ t½. Type it, with its unit.", "switch_ask": "ln 2 = 0.693. Keep the time unit of the half-life.", "notebook_prompt": "Show me how to find λ from a half-life. Use an isotope other than mine. Do not solve my problem. Then give me one short practice problem and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What is the unit of λ?", "park_text": "\\( \\lambda = \\ln 2 / t_{1/2} \\)", "status": "approved (instructor, 18 Oct 2026)"}, "fraction_found": {"state": "fraction_found", "stage": "calculate", "label_when_done": "Fraction found", "ask": "Find the fraction remaining, \\( N_t/N_0 = e^{-\\lambda t} \\). Type it.", "switch_ask": "Use the same time unit as λ. Use e, not 10.", "notebook_prompt": "Show me how to find the fraction remaining after a time. Use numbers other than mine. Do not solve my problem. Then give me one short practice problem and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "Is your answer what remains, or what decayed?", "park_text": "\\( N_t/N_0 = e^{-\\lambda t} \\)", "status": "approved (instructor, 18 Oct 2026)"}, "mass_found": {"state": "mass_found", "stage": "calculate", "label_when_done": "Mass left found", "ask": "Find the mass left. Type it in grams.", "switch_ask": "Fraction remaining times the starting mass.", "notebook_prompt": "Explain converting the fraction remaining to a mass. Do not solve my problem. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What multiplies the fraction?", "park_text": "mass left = (Nt/N0) × m0", "status": "approved (instructor, 18 Oct 2026)"}, "ratio_found": {"state": "ratio_found", "stage": "calculate", "label_when_done": "Ratio found", "ask": "Find Nt/N0 (amounts, masses or activities: they are proportional). Type it.", "switch_ask":